and two valid messages \emph{m}0\emph{, m}1, is given an encryption of
\emph{mb} under \emph{id∗}for \emph{b ←\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}} chosen
uniformly at random, and attempts to guess the value of \emph{b} without
ever querying its oracle on identity \emph{id∗}(queries can be

made both before and after the adversary produces \emph{id∗, m}0\emph{,
m}1). We say that the scheme is secure if every

\begin{quote}
PPT adversary succeeds with probability at most negligibly more than
1\emph{/}2.

