\begin{quote}
In this section, we demonstrate trapdoors for certain families of random
lattices that, roughly speaking, enjoy worst-case hardness. We then
develop some foundational tools and primitives that our cryptographic
applications will build upon.

\textbf{5.1} \textbf{Hard Random Lattices}

We start by giving a unified description of two related families of
random lattices that have appeared in recent works. Both families
consist of integer lattices (i.e., subsets of Z\emph{m}) that are
invariant under shifts by \emph{q} in each of the coordinates, for some
specified integer modulus \emph{q}. In other words, whether a vector
\textbf{x} \emph{∈}Z\emph{m}belongs to the lattice is determined
entirely by the entries of \textbf{x} modulo \emph{q}.
\end{quote}

natural security parameter and all other variables are functions of
\emph{n}; for example, \emph{m} = \emph{m}(\emph{n}) is typically In
more detail, let \textbf{A} \emph{∈}Z\emph{n×m} for some positive
integers \emph{n, m, q}. In this work (as in prior ones), \emph{n} is
the

\emph{O}(\emph{n} log \emph{n}), and the modulus \emph{q} =
\emph{q}(\emph{n}) is some small polynomial, e.g., \emph{O}(\emph{n}3).
We consider two kinds of full-rank \emph{m}-dimensional integer lattices
defined by \textbf{A}. The first consists of those integer vectors that
are``orthogonal'' (modulo \emph{q}) to the rows of \textbf{A}, and is
defined as

Λ\emph{⊥}(\textbf{A}) = \emph{\{}\textbf{e} \emph{∈}Z\emph{m}:
\textbf{Ae} = \textbf{0} mod \emph{q\}.}

\begin{quote}
The second lattice is generated by the (transposed) rows of \textbf{A},
and is defined as
\end{quote}

Λ(\textbf{A}) = \emph{\{}\textbf{y} \emph{∈}Z\emph{m}: \textbf{y} =
\textbf{A}\emph{T}\textbf{s} mod \emph{q} for some \textbf{s}
\emph{∈}Z\emph{n\}.}

In the terminology of coding theory, \textbf{A} is the ``parity check''
matrix for the lattice Λ\emph{⊥}(\textbf{A}), and \textbf{A}\emph{T}is
the``generator matrix'' for the lattice Λ(\textbf{A}). When \textbf{A}
is implicit from context, we sometimes omit it as an argument and just
write Λ\emph{⊥}and Λ.

\begin{quote}
Throughout the paper, we use two easy but important facts about the
lattices defined above. First, it can be seen from their definitions
that Λ and Λ\emph{⊥}(appropriately scaled) are duals:
\end{quote}

