The hard-on-average problem first proposed by Ajtai {[}Ajt96{]} is to
find a short nonzero integer solution \textbf{e} \emph{∈}Z\emph{m}to the
homogeneous linear system \textbf{Ae} = \textbf{0} mod \emph{q} for
uniformly random \textbf{A} \emph{∈}Z\emph{n×m} . This is

\begin{quote}
syntactically equivalent to finding some short nonzero vector in
Λ\emph{⊥}(\textbf{A}). The problem was formalized as follows in
{[}MR07{]}.
\end{quote}

\textbf{Definition 5.5.} The \emph{small integer solution} problem SIS
(in the \emph{ℓ}2 norm) is as follows: given an integer \emph{q}, a
matrix \textbf{A} \emph{∈}Z\emph{n×m} , and a real \emph{β}, find a
\emph{nonzero} integer vector \textbf{e} \emph{∈}Z\emph{m}such that
\textbf{Ae} = \textbf{0} mod \emph{q} and\emph{∥}\textbf{e}\emph{∥}2
\emph{≤β}. For functions \emph{q}(\emph{n}), \emph{m}(\emph{n}), and
\emph{β}(\emph{n}), SIS\emph{q,m,β} is the ensemble over instances
(\emph{q}(\emph{n})\emph{,} \textbf{A}\emph{, β}(\emph{n})) where
\textbf{A} \emph{∈}Z\emph{n×m}(\emph{n}) is uniformly random.

\begin{quote}
We now define a variant problem, which is to find a short solution to a
random \emph{inhomogeneous} system, specifically, \textbf{Ae} =
\textbf{u} mod \emph{q} (where both \textbf{A} and \textbf{u} are
uniformly random).
\end{quote}

\textbf{Definition 5.6.} The \emph{inhomogeneous small integer solution}
problem ISIS (in the \emph{ℓ}2 norm) is as follows: given an integer
\emph{q}, a matrix \textbf{A} \emph{∈}Z\emph{n×m} , a syndrome
\textbf{u} \emph{∈}Z\emph{n q}, and a real \emph{β}, find an integer
vector \textbf{e} \emph{∈}Z\emph{m} such that \textbf{Ae} = \textbf{u}
mod \emph{q} and \emph{∥}\textbf{e}\emph{∥}2 \emph{≤β}. The average-case
problem ISIS\emph{q,m,β} is defined similarly, where \textbf{A} and
\textbf{u} are uniformly random and independent.

\begin{quote}
The ISIS problem is phrased as a \emph{syndrome decoding} problem, and
is equivalent to the problem of decoding an arbitrary integer target
point \textbf{t} \emph{∈}Z\emph{m}to within distance \emph{β} on the
lattice Λ\emph{⊥}= Λ\emph{⊥}(\textbf{A}). Specifically, the target
point's syndrome is \textbf{u} = \textbf{At} mod \emph{q}, and solving
ISIS on this syndrome yields a short error vector \textbf{e}
\emph{∈}Z\emph{m}having the same syndrome \textbf{u}. This error vector
yields a lattice point \textbf{v} = \textbf{t} \emph{−}\textbf{e}
\emph{∈}Λ\emph{⊥}, because
\end{quote}

19

\textbf{Av} = \textbf{At} \emph{−}\textbf{Ae} = \textbf{0} mod \emph{q};
furthermore, \textbf{v} is within distance \emph{β} of \textbf{t}.
Conversely, decoding \textbf{t} to some \textbf{v}
\emph{∈}Λ\emph{⊥}within distance \emph{β} yields an error vector
\textbf{e} = \textbf{t} \emph{−}\textbf{v} such that \textbf{Ae} =
\textbf{u} mod \emph{q}. In this work it will be more convenient and
efficient to work with syndrome decoding, because the lattice
Λ\emph{⊥}is ``modded out'' of the instances. In other words, an instance
of ISIS refers only to a particular coset of Λ\emph{⊥}in Z\emph{n q},
rather than an unrestricted target point in Z\emph{m}.

\begin{quote}
Of course, the SIS and ISIS problems are only meaningful if they admit
valid solutions for the particular choices of \emph{q}, \emph{m},
\emph{β}. For \emph{β ≥√m} and \emph{m ≥}2\emph{n} lg \emph{q} (for
prime \emph{q}), Lemma 5.1 implies that with overwhelming probability
over the choice of \textbf{A}, there is an \textbf{e}
\emph{∈\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}m}such that \textbf{Ae} = \textbf{u} mod
\emph{q} for any \textbf{u} \emph{∈}Z\emph{n q}. Then because
\emph{∥}\textbf{e}\emph{∥≤√m ≤β}, we see that a uniformly random
instance of ISIS\emph{q,m,β} has a solution with overwhelming
probability. For the same parameters, a pigeonhole argument shows that
SIS \emph{always} admits a nonzero solution (even for non-prime
\emph{q}, though we will not need this fact). From now on, \emph{q},
\emph{m}, and \emph{β} will always implicitly satisfy the above
contraints.

Using Gaussian techniques, Micciancio and Regev {[}MR07{]} showed that
the SIS\emph{q,m,β} problem is as hard (on the average) as approximating
certain worst-case problems on lattices to within small factors. We give
a simpler and slightly tighter proof (also showing hardness for ISIS)
that employs our discrete Gaussian sampling algorithm, and which works
for a smaller modulus \emph{q}.

\textbf{Proposition 5.7.} \emph{For any poly-bounded m, β} =
\emph{poly}(\emph{n}) \emph{and for any prime q ≥β ·ω}(\emph{√n} log
\emph{n})\emph{, the average-case problems} SIS\emph{q,m,β and}
ISIS\emph{q,m,β are as hard as approximating the} SIVP \emph{problem
(among others) in the worst case to within certain γ} = \emph{β
·}˜\emph{O}(\emph{√n}) \emph{factors.}8

Note that Proposition 5.7 gives a ``sliding scale'' of hardness (and
modulus \emph{q}) depending on the value of\emph{β}. For the tightest
value of \emph{β} =\emph{√m}, we can take \emph{q} = ˜\emph{O}(\emph{n})
and obtain approximation factors \emph{γ} = ˜\emph{O}(\emph{n}) for the
worst-case problems. However, for our trapdoor functions and other
cryptographic primitives, we will need to assume hardness of ISIS for
larger values of \emph{β} (e.g., \emph{β ≈m}1\emph{.}5= ˜trapdoor
inversion algorithm is only able to produce preimages of length
approximately\emph{√m} times the length \emph{O}(\emph{n}1\emph{.}5)).
This is because our

of the trapdoor basis; the shorter the basis, the smaller we may take
\emph{β} to be, and the weaker the underlying assumptions can be.

The proof of Proposition 5.7 appears in Section 9.

\textbf{5.3} \textbf{Preimage Sampleable Functions}

\textbf{5.3.1} \textbf{Definitions}
\end{quote}

We start by defining some enhanced variants of preimage sampleable
(trapdoor) functions, which are given by a tuple of probabilistic
polynomial-time algorithms (TrapGen\emph{,} SampleDom\emph{,}
SamplePre).

\begin{quote}
A collection of one-way \emph{preimage sampleable functions} (PSFs)
satisfies the following:

1. \emph{Generating a function with trapdoor}: TrapGen(1\emph{n})
outputs (\emph{a, t}), where \emph{a} is the description of an
efficiently-computable function \emph{fa} : \emph{Dn →Rn} (for some
efficiently-recognizable domain \emph{Dn} and range \emph{Rn} depending
on \emph{n}), and \emph{t} is some trapdoor information for \emph{fa}.

For the remaining properties, fix some (\emph{a, t})
\emph{←}TrapGen(1\emph{n}).

8It is possible to base the hardness of ISIS solely on the assumed
hardness of SIS, but we only know of such a reduction for slightly
looser values of \emph{β}. Because ISIS is interesting on its own, and
might even be harder than SIS, we elect to treat it as a separate
problem and give a direct, tight reduction from worst-case lattice
problems.
\end{quote}

20

\begin{quote}
2. \emph{Domain sampling with uniform output}: SampleDom(1\emph{n})
samples an \emph{x} from some (possibly non-

uniform) distribution over \emph{Dn}, for which the distribution of
\emph{fa}(\emph{x}) is uniform over \emph{Rn}.

3. \emph{Preimage sampling with trapdoor}: for every \emph{y ∈Rn},
SamplePre(\emph{t, y}) samples from the conditional distribution of
\emph{x ←}SampleDom(1\emph{n}), given \emph{fa}(\emph{x}) = \emph{y}.

4. \emph{One-wayness without trapdoor}: for any probabilistic poly-time
algorithm \emph{A}, the probability that \emph{A}(1\emph{n, a, y})
\emph{∈f−}1 \emph{a}(\emph{y}) \emph{⊆Dn}is negligible, where the
probability is taken over the choice of \emph{a}, the target value
\emph{y ←Rn} chosen uniformly at random, and \emph{A}'s random coins.9
\end{quote}

Note that trapdoor \emph{permutations} (with uniform distribution over
the domain) satisfy this definition, because

\begin{quote}
the output distribution is uniform and every point has a unique inverse.

A collection of \emph{collision-resistant} preimage sampleable functions
satisfies the above properties, plus the

following:

5. \emph{Preimage min-entropy}: for every \emph{y ∈Rn}, the conditional
min-entropy of \emph{x ←}SampleDom(1\emph{n}) given \emph{fa}(\emph{x})
= \emph{y} is at least \emph{ω}(log \emph{n}).

6. \emph{Collision resistance without trapdoor}: for any probabilistic
poly-time algorithm \emph{A}, the probability that \emph{A}(1\emph{n,
a}) outputs distinct \emph{x, x′∈Dn} such that \emph{fa}(\emph{x}) =
\emph{fa}(\emph{x′}) is negligible, where the probability is taken over
the choice of \emph{a} and \emph{A}'s random coins.
\end{quote}

We point out that these two additional properties together imply
one-wayness (Property 4). For if not, then

given a function \emph{fa} one could find a collision as follows: choose
an \emph{x ←}SampleDom(1\emph{n}), and obtain a preimage \emph{x′}of
\emph{fa}(\emph{x}) from the adversarial inverter. Then because \emph{x}
has large min-entropy given \emph{fa}(\emph{x}), we

\begin{quote}
have \emph{x′̸}= \emph{x} with overwhelming probability, so \emph{x,
x′}form a collision.

It is also possible to define a trapdoor variant of \emph{universal
one-wayness} {[}NY89{]}, which is implied by
\end{quote}

collision resistance. Because our constructions will be
collision-resistant anyway, we omit a precise definition.

\begin{quote}
A collection of \emph{claw-free} pairs of one-way/collision-resistant
PSFs is defined similarly, with the following
\end{quote}

differences: TrapGen outputs a pair \emph{a, a′}describing functions
\emph{fa, fa′} : \emph{Dn →Rn} (respectively), and their respective
trapdoors \emph{t, t′}. The preimage sampler works the same way for both
\emph{fa} (given \emph{t}) and \emph{fa′} (given

\begin{quote}
\emph{t′}). The hardness condition is that no PPT algorithm \emph{A},
given \emph{a, a′}, can find a pair \emph{x, x′∈Dn} such that
\emph{fa}(\emph{x}) = \emph{fa′}(\emph{x′}). Each function \emph{fa,
fa′} may itself also be collision-resistant in the usual way.

\textbf{A needed relaxation.} To be completely precise, the trapdoor
functions we construct will actually satisfy a
\end{quote}

slightly relaxed definition in which the properties are satisfied only
\emph{statistically}. First, the properties will

hold only with overwhelming probability over the choice of \emph{a}.
Additionally, SampleDom(1\emph{n}) will output an

\emph{x ∈Dn} only with overwhelming probability, and the distribution of
\emph{fa}(\emph{x}) will only be statistically close to uniform.
Finally, SamplePre will sample from a distribution over the preimages
that is statistically close to

\begin{quote}
the prescribed conditional distribution. None of these relaxations will
affect security in our applications.

