\multicolumn{2}{>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.6667} + 2\tabcolsep}@{}}{%
\begin{minipage}[t]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\begin{quote}
\textbf{Signature Schemes}
\end{quote}
\end{minipage}} \\
\bottomrule()
\end{longtable}

\begin{quote}
The hash-and-sign paradigm for signature schemes, first suggested in
{[}DH76{]}, works as follows: the public verification key is a trapdoor
function \emph{f} and the signing key is \emph{f−}1. To sign a message
\emph{m}, first hash \emph{m} to some point \emph{y} =
\emph{H}(\emph{m}) in the range of a trapdoor function \emph{f}, then
output the signature \emph{σ} = \emph{f−}1(\emph{y}). To verify
(\emph{m, σ}), simply check that \emph{f}(\emph{σ}) =
\emph{H}(\emph{m}). Bellare and Rogaway {[}BR93{]} formalized this
notion and showed that this basic scheme, called Full-Domain Hash (FDH),
is existentially unforgeable under chosen-message attacks when \emph{f}
is a trapdoor \emph{permutation} and the hash function \emph{H} is
modelled as a random oracle. Many variations on this theme have been
proposed, such as the Probabilistic FDH (PFDH) scheme of Coron
{[}Cor00{]} and the Probabilistic Signature Scheme (PSS) of Bellare and
Rogaway {[}BR96{]}. These were proposed in part to improve upon the
\emph{exact} security of FDH, which is quite loose when instantiated
with a black-box trapdoor permutation (the success probability of the
reduction is a \emph{Q}hash factor smaller than that of the forger,
where \emph{Q}hash is the number of hash queries made by the forger).

All of the above schemes were originally intended to be instantiated
with trapdoor \emph{permutations}, such as RSA. In this section, we show
that they can be instantiated securely using our notion of preimage
sampleable functions. In fact, we are even able to give a \emph{tight}
security reduction for FDH by exploiting \emph{collision resistance}.
This stands in constrast to the best known reductions for FDH using
trapdoor permutations: for trapdoor permutations treated as a black-box,
the reduction \emph{must} lose a factor of \emph{Q}hash {[}DR02{]}; for
RSA and claw-free permutations, the known reductions still lose a factor
of \emph{Q}sign {[}Cor00, DR02{]}). In addition, all of our
instantiations are \emph{strongly} unforgeable.

\textbf{6.1} \textbf{Full-Domain Hash Scheme}
\end{quote}

We start with a version of the FDH signature scheme using trapdoor
\emph{collision-resistant} PSFs; recall that such a collection can be
constructed assuming that SIS is hard on the average for appropriate
parameters. In order

23

for our security reduction to work, the signer must give out \emph{at
most one} preimage of a given point. This can be implemented by making
the signer \emph{stateful}, or by using a pseudorandom function (e.g.,
the random oracle itself) to implement ``repeatable randomness'' in a
standard way. The PRF is used to generate the random coins of the
preimage sampler, so if the sampler's randomness complexity is large,
this solution may be impractical and the PFDH scheme below may be a
better option. For simplicity, we describe the stateful version of the
scheme.

\begin{quote}
The scheme is built upon a collection of collision-resistant PSFs given
by (TrapGen\emph{,} SampleDom\emph{,} SamplePre), and operates relative
to a function \emph{H} = \emph{Hn} : \emph{\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗→Rn}
that is modelled as a random oracle (recall that \emph{Dn} and \emph{Rn}
are the efficiently-recognizable domain and range, respectively, of the
collection for security parameter \emph{n}).

\emph{•} SigKeyGen(1\emph{n}): let (\emph{a, t})
\emph{←}TrapGen(1\emph{n}), where \emph{a} describes a function
\emph{fa} and \emph{t} is its trapdoor. The verification key is \emph{a}
and the signing key is \emph{t}.

\emph{•} Sign(\emph{t, m}): if (\emph{m, σm}) is in local storage,
output \emph{σm}. Else, let \emph{σm ←}SamplePre(\emph{t, H}(\emph{m})),
store (\emph{m, σm}), and output \emph{σm}.

\emph{•} Verify(\emph{a, m, σ}): if \emph{σ ∈Dn} and \emph{fa}(\emph{σ})
= \emph{H}(\emph{m}), accept. Else, reject.

\textbf{Proposition 6.1.} \emph{The scheme described above is strongly
existentially unforgeable under a chosen-message attack.}

\emph{Proof.} It is clear that the scheme is complete, by the properties
of the trapdoor collection.

Assume, for contradiction, that there is an adversary \emph{A} that
breaks the existential unforgeability of the signature scheme with
probability \emph{ϵ} = \emph{ϵ}(\emph{n}). We construct a poly-time
adversary \emph{S} that breaks the trapdoor collision-resistant hash
function with probability negligibly close to \emph{ϵ}. Given an index
\emph{a} describing a function \emph{fa}, \emph{S} runs \emph{A} on
public key \emph{a}, and simulates the random oracle \emph{H} and
signing oracle as follows. Without loss of generality, assume that
\emph{A} queries \emph{H} on every message \emph{m} before making a
signing query on \emph{m}. \emph{•} For every query to \emph{H} on a
distinct \emph{m ∈\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗}, \emph{S} lets \emph{σm
←}SampleDom(1\emph{n}), stores (\emph{m, σm}), and returns
\emph{fa}(\emph{σm}) to \emph{A}. (If \emph{H} was previously queried on
\emph{m}, \emph{S} looks up (\emph{m, σm}) and returns
\emph{fa}(\emph{σm}).) \emph{•} Whenever \emph{A} makes a signing query
on \emph{m}, \emph{S} looks up (\emph{m, σm}) in its local storage and
returns \emph{σm} as the signature.

Now without loss of generality, assume that before outputting its
attempted forgery (\emph{m∗, σ∗}), \emph{A} queries \emph{H} on
\emph{m∗}. When \emph{A} produces (\emph{m∗, σ∗}), \emph{S} looks up
(\emph{m∗, σm∗}) in its local storage and outputs (\emph{σ∗, σm∗}) as a
collision in \emph{fa}.

We now analyze the reduction. First, we claim that the view of \emph{A}
in the real chosen-message attack is identical to its view as provided
by \emph{S}. (This assumes that the trapdoor function properties from
Section 5.3.1 are \emph{perfect}; if they are only statistical, the
views are statistically close.) For each distinct query \emph{m} to
\emph{H}, the value returned by \emph{S} is \emph{fa}(\emph{σm}) where
\emph{σm ←}SampleDom(1\emph{n}); by the ``uniform output'' property of
the collection, this is identical to the uniformly random value of
\emph{H}(\emph{m}) \emph{∈Rn} in the real system. Now fix the value
\emph{H}(\emph{m}). Then for every signature query on the message
\emph{m}, \emph{S} returns a single value \emph{σm} which is distributed
as SampleDom(1\emph{n}), given \emph{fa}(\emph{σm}) =
\emph{H}(\emph{m}). In the real system, signature queries on \emph{m}
(even repeated ones) are answered by a single value having the same
distribution, by the preimage sampleability of SamplePre.
\end{quote}

Therefore \emph{A} outputs a valid forgery (\emph{m∗, σ∗}) with
probability (negligibly close to) \emph{ϵ}. Because \emph{σ∗}is a valid
signature on \emph{m∗}, we have \emph{σ∗∈Dn} and \emph{fa}(\emph{σ∗}) =
\emph{H}(\emph{m∗}) = \emph{fa}(\emph{σm∗}). It simply remains to check
that\emph{σ∗̸}= \emph{σm∗}, i.e., that they form a collision in
\emph{fa}. There are two cases to consider:

24

\begin{quote}
1. If \emph{A} made a signature query on \emph{m∗}, it received back the
signature \emph{σm∗}. Because (\emph{m∗, σ∗}) is considered a forgery,
we have \emph{σ∗̸}= \emph{σm∗}.

2. If \emph{A} did \emph{not} make a signature query on \emph{m∗}, then
for the query to \emph{H} on \emph{m∗}, \emph{S} stored a tuple
(\emph{m∗, σm∗}) for \emph{σm∗←}SampleDom(1\emph{n}), and returned
\emph{fa}(\emph{σm∗}) to \emph{A}. By the preimage min-entropy property
of the hash family, the min-entropy of \emph{σm∗}given
\emph{fa}(\emph{σm∗}) (and the rest of the view of \emph{A}, which is
independent of \emph{σm∗}) is \emph{ω}(log \emph{n}). Thus, the
signature \emph{σ∗̸}= \emph{σm∗}, except with negligible probability
2\emph{−ω}(log \emph{n}).
\end{quote}

