\begin{quote}
The PFDH scheme replaces the statefulness of FDH with a random ``salt''
for each signature, and is parame-terized by the length \emph{k} of the
salt (for simplicity, we can set \emph{k} = \emph{n}, though any
\emph{k} = \emph{ω}(log \emph{n}) will suffice for asymptotic security).

\emph{•} SigKeyGen(1\emph{n}): let (\emph{a, t})
\emph{←}TrapGen(1\emph{n}), where \emph{a} describes a function
\emph{fa} and \emph{t} is its trapdoor. The verification key is \emph{a}
and the signing key is \emph{t}.

\emph{•} Sign(\emph{t, m}): choose \emph{r ←\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}k}at
random, let \emph{σ ←}SamplePre(\emph{t, H}(\emph{m∥r})), and output
(\emph{r, σ}).

\emph{•} Verify(\emph{a, m,} (\emph{r, σ})): if \emph{σ ∈Dn} and \emph{r
∈\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}k}and \emph{fa}(\emph{σ}) = \emph{H}(\emph{m∥r}),
then accept. Else, reject.

\textbf{Proposition 6.2.} \emph{The scheme described above is strongly
existentially unforgeable under a chosen-message attack.}
\end{quote}

\emph{Proof.} The proof is almost identical the prior one, so we simply
describe the main idea. Security can be based on either
\emph{collision-resistance} as above (which can be based on the hardness
of SIS), or on \emph{claw-free}

\begin{longtable}[]{@{}
