\begin{minipage}[t]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\begin{quote}
\textbf{7}
\end{quote}
\end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[t]{\linewidth}\raggedright
\begin{quote}
\textbf{Identity-Based Encryption}
\end{quote}
\end{minipage} \\
\bottomrule()
\end{longtable}

In this section we construct an identity-based encryption (IBE) system
based on the LWE problem, in the random oracle model. (We also show that
a similar system is secure in the plain model, under an
``interactive''assumption relating to LWE.)\\
As we show in Section 8, there is a variant of Regev's cryptosystem
{[}Reg05{]} in which a single latticeΛ(\textbf{A}) can support the
public keys of many users, and a trapdoor for \textbf{A} enables
extraction of the secret key from any well-formed public key. At first
glance, it may seem that this is all that is needed for an
identity-based cryptosystem. However, the situation is not so simple.
Well-formed public keys in that system are \emph{exponentially sparse},
because they correspond to points very close to the lattice
Λ(\textbf{A}). It is not at all clear how a hash function or random
oracle could securely map identities to valid public keys.

\begin{quote}
To remedy this situation, we construct a ``dual'' of Regev's
cryptosystem, in which the generation and encryption algorithms are
essentially swapped. More specifically, in the dual system, the secret
key is a vector \textbf{e} distributed according to \emph{D}Z\emph{m,r},
and the corresponding public key is its syndrome \textbf{u} =
\emph{f}\textbf{A}(\textbf{e}) \emph{∈}Z\emph{n q}.
\end{quote}

25

\begin{quote}
The encryption algorithm chooses a pseudorandom LWE vector \textbf{p} =
\textbf{A}\emph{T}\textbf{s} + \textbf{x} (for a uniform secret
\textbf{s} \emph{∈}Z\emph{n} and error vector \textbf{x} \emph{←χm}),
and uses the syndrome \textbf{u} to generate one more LWE instance
\emph{p} = \textbf{u}\emph{T}\textbf{s} + \emph{x} as a ``pad'' to hide
the message (where \emph{x ←χ}). Because the public key syndrome
\textbf{u} is statistically close to uniform, the adversary's view in
the dual system is indistinguishable from uniform, under the hardness of
LWE. For the same reason, the scheme (and its identity-based version
below) is also \emph{anonymous}; that is, a ciphertext hides the
identity to which it was encrypted.

The crucial feature of the dual cryptosystem is that its public keys are
\emph{dense}; in fact, every syndrome \emph{q}is a valid public key that
has many essentially equivalent decryption keys \textbf{e}
\emph{∈}Z\emph{m q}. We therefore \textbf{u} \emph{∈}Z\emph{n}\\
have all the properties we need to implement an IBE system. Identities
are hashed to syndromes in Z\emph{n q}, with the assurance that every
such syndrome is a well-defined public key for the dual scheme.
Furthermore, a trapdoor for \textbf{A} allows a trusted authority to
efficiently sample a secret key \textbf{e} corresponding to any syndrome
\textbf{u}, under the same distribution as in the dual cryptosystem.

We first describe the dual cryptosystem and prove that it is secure
based on the hardness of LWE. We then show how to use this to construct
an IBE system.

\textbf{7.1} \textbf{Dual Cryptosystem}\\
Our public-key dual cryptosystem is defined below. It is parameterized
