\emph{Proof.} First we show completeness. Note that for any identity
\emph{id} and its syndrome \textbf{u} = \emph{H}(\emph{id}), IBEExtract

samples from \emph{f−}1 \textbf{A}(\textbf{u}) and produces a secret key
\textbf{e} whose distribution is statistically close to that of a secret

key for the public key \textbf{u} in the dual cryptosystem. Therefore
IBEDec decrypts correctly as long as DualDec

\begin{quote}
does. Furthermore, the system is anonymous because IBEEnc simply returns
the output of DualEnc.

We now show semantic security in the random oracle model. Let \emph{A}
be a PPT adversary that attacks the IBE scheme and has advantage
\emph{ϵ} using \emph{Q}hash distinct queries to \emph{H}. We will
construct an adversary \emph{S} that attacks the dual cryptosystem by
simulating the view of \emph{A}, and has advantage negligibly close to
\emph{ϵ/Q}hash. The adversary \emph{S} works as follows. On input a
shared matrix \textbf{A} \emph{∈}Z\emph{n×m} and a public key
\textbf{u}\emph{∗∈}Z\emph{n q}for
\end{quote}

the dual cryptosystem, \emph{S} chooses an index \emph{i
←}{[}\emph{Q}hash{]} uniformly at random and simulates the view of
\emph{A} as follows:

\begin{quote}
\emph{• Hash queries}: on \emph{A}'s \emph{j}th distinct query
\emph{idj} to \emph{H}, do the following: if \emph{j} = \emph{i}, then
locally store the tuple (\emph{idj,} \textbf{u}\emph{∗, ⊥}) and return
\textbf{u}\emph{∗}to \emph{A}. Otherwise for \emph{j ̸}= \emph{i},
generate a public/secret key pair (\textbf{u}\emph{j,}
\textbf{e}\emph{j}) \emph{←}DualKeyGen, locally store the tuple
(\emph{idj,} \textbf{u}\emph{j,} \textbf{e}\emph{j}), and return
\textbf{u}\emph{j} to \emph{A}.

\emph{• Secret key queries}: when \emph{A} asks for a secret key for the
identity \emph{id}, assume without loss of generality that \emph{A}
already queried \emph{H} on \emph{id}. Retrieve the unique tuple
(\emph{id,} \textbf{u}\emph{,} \textbf{e}) from local storage. If
\textbf{e} = \emph{⊥}, then output a random bit and abort, otherwise
return \textbf{e} to \emph{A}.

\emph{• Challenge ciphertext}: when \emph{A} produces a challenge
identity \emph{id∗}(distinct from all its secret key queries) and
messages \emph{m}0\emph{, m}1, assume without loss of generality that
\emph{A} already queried \emph{H} on \emph{id∗}. If \emph{id∗̸}=
\emph{idi}, i.e., if the tuple (\emph{id∗,} \textbf{u}\emph{∗, ⊥}) is
not in local storage, then output a random bit and abort. Otherwise,
relay the messages \emph{m}0\emph{, m}1 to the challenger, receive a
challenge ciphertext \emph{c∗}, and return \emph{c∗}to \emph{A}.
\end{quote}

When \emph{A} terminates with some output, \emph{S} terminates with the
same output.

