\begin{quote}
The main idea for proving security of the IBE under the interactive
assumption is as follows: the simulator
\end{quote}

is given \textbf{A}, \textbf{p}, hash function \emph{H}, and access to
the signing oracle. It simulates an IBE system having public

28

parameter \textbf{A} and \emph{H}, and answers secret key queries by
simply querying the signing oracle. When given the challenge identity
\emph{id∗}and challenge message \emph{m}0\emph{, m}1, the simulator
additionally queries the signing oracle on \emph{id∗}and obtains a
secret key \textbf{e}\emph{∗}. It then constructs a challenge ciphertext
by encrypting a random \emph{mb}``with the secret key
\textbf{e}\emph{∗}.'' Specifically, the ciphertext is made up of
\textbf{p} and a ``pad'' \textbf{e}\emph{T}\textbf{p} \emph{∈}Z\emph{q}
that hides the message \emph{mb} in the standard way. If \textbf{p} is
uniform, it can be shown that the pad is essentially uniform and
independent of the other variables, thus the adversary has no advantage.
If \textbf{p} = \textbf{A}\emph{T}\textbf{s} + \textbf{x} is an LWE
instance, the ciphertext is distributed as in the real IBE system. Note
that the simulated pad \textbf{e}\emph{T}\textbf{p} =
\textbf{u}\emph{T}\textbf{s} + \textbf{e}\emph{T}\textbf{x} is
correlated with the length \emph{∥}\textbf{x}\emph{∥}; therefore, the
encryption algorithm in the real IBE system will also choose a pad using
an error distribution whose standard deviation is determined by
\emph{∥}\textbf{x}\emph{∥}. The full proof of security is somewhat
subtle, and is more convenient when the error terms in the LWE problem
are \emph{continuous} quantities, not discrete (this is actually the
main form of the problem studied in {[}Reg05{]}). When ``encrypting with
the secret key,'' the simulator also needs to add a small amount of
continuous Gaussian error to the pad \textbf{e}\emph{T}\textbf{p}, in
order to ensure that its overall distribution is close to a continuous
Gaussian; this technique is also used in the main reduction of
{[}Reg05{]}. We defer the details.

\begin{quote}
\textbf{8} \textbf{Trapdoors for Learning with Errors}

Here we demonstrate some trapdoor techniques for the learning with
errors (LWE) problem {[}Reg05{]} and certain cryptosystems based upon
it. Some of these techniques have been applied in a concurrent work of
Peikert, Vaikuntanathan, and Waters {[}PVW07{]} to construct efficient
and universally composable oblivious transfer protocols based on LWE.

\textbf{8.1} \textbf{Variant Cryptosystem}

We start with a slight variant of Regev's cryptosystem {[}Reg05{]},
which differs only in the encryption algorithm. The original algorithm
chooses a uniformly random binary vector \textbf{e} \emph{∈\{}0\emph{,}
1\emph{\}m}(corresponding to a random subset of \emph{\{}1\emph{, . . .
, m\}}) and computes the corresponding subset sum \textbf{Ae} of the
columns of \textbf{A} \emph{∈}Z\emph{n×m} . Our encryption algorithm
instead chooses a random vector \textbf{e} from the discrete Gaussian
\emph{D}Z\emph{m,r} over the integer lattice Z\emph{m}(using the SampleD
algorithm from Section 4), and computes the syndrome \textbf{Ae}. The
particular value of the Gaussian parameter \emph{r} will be a parameter
of the scheme, and can be chosen based on the needs of the particular
application.

We define an optimized version of the cryptosystem (also described in
{[}Reg05{]}) in which all users share a matrix \textbf{A}
\emph{∈}Z\emph{n×m} chosen uniformly at random by some trusted source.
All operations are performed over Z\emph{q}.

\emph{•} LWEKeyGen: choose a secret decryption key \textbf{s}
\emph{←}Z\emph{n q}uniformly at random. The public key is the vector
\textbf{p} = \textbf{A}\emph{T}\textbf{s} + \textbf{x} \emph{∈}Z\emph{m
q}, where each \emph{xi}is chosen independently from the error
distribution \emph{χ} for \emph{i ∈}{[}\emph{m}{]}.

