keys \textbf{p} generated by LWEKeyGen typically have large
\emph{δ}(\textbf{p}).

Messy keys (though not named as such) have played a crucial role in the
security proofs for prior lattice-based cryptosystems {[}AD97, Reg04b,
Reg05{]}. In those works it was sufficient to show that \emph{most} keys
are messy, without necessarily identifying their particular
characteristics. This was always done via non-constructive probabilistic
arguments.

In the proof of security for the oblivious transfer protocol in
{[}PVW07{]}, the simulator needs to \emph{identify messy keys
efficiently} (with the help of a trapdoor). This calls for an
\emph{explicit} condition that identifies such
