\begin{quote}
The actual algorithm and its analysis depend crucially on the
relationship between the Gaussian parameter \emph{r} used in encryption
and the Gaussian parameter \emph{s} of the distribution over Λ\emph{∗}.
The smaller \emph{s} is, the smaller
\end{quote}

31

\begin{quote}
we can make \emph{r} and still correctly identify messy keys. In turn, a
smaller value of \emph{r} means that we can use a larger LWE error
parameter \emph{α} and still maintain correctness of the cryptosystem.
Finally, a larger value of \emph{α}corresponds to tighter approximation
factors for worst-case lattice problems under the quantum reduction of
{[}Reg05{]}.

We remark that we only know how to identify messy keys for the
\emph{single-bit} cryptosystem that uses one public key vector
\textbf{p} ,as opposed to the more efficient multi-bit system that uses
multiple vectors \textbf{p}\emph{i}, constructed in {[}PVW07{]}. The
reason is in the multi-bit system, the key is messy if the minimum
distance remains large after adjoining \emph{all} the
\textbf{p}\emph{i}s to the common lattice Λ(\textbf{A}). Testing for
this condition (at least naively) seems to require checking all of the
exponentially-many integer combinations of the \textbf{p}\emph{i}s.
\end{quote}

We start by recalling the algorithm of {[}AR05{]} that distinguishes
between points that are far from a latticeΛ and those that are close to
it, given access to Gaussian samples over the dual lattice Λ\emph{∗}.
The meanings of``far'' and ``close'' are determined by the Gaussian
parameter \emph{s}.

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Proposition 8.5} ({[}AR05, Pei07{]})\textbf{.} \emph{There is a
deterministic polynomial-time oracle machine VOthat, given a full-rank
lattice} Λ \emph{⊂}R\emph{m(specified by an arbitrary basis) and a
point} \textbf{x} \emph{∈}R\emph{m, and given access to an oracle O that
samples from the distribution D}Λ\emph{∗,s for some s \textgreater{}}
0\emph{, has the following behavior:}\\
\emph{• If} dist\emph{∞}(\textbf{x}\emph{,} Λ)
\emph{≤}1\emph{/}(10\emph{s√m})\emph{, then V outputs ``close,'' except
with probability} 2\emph{−}Ω(\emph{m})\emph{.}

\emph{• If} dist\emph{∞}(\textbf{x}\emph{,} Λ) \emph{≥√}log \emph{m/s,
then V outputs ``far,'' except with probability}
2\emph{−}Ω(\emph{m})\emph{.}

\emph{The probabilities are taken over the samples produced by the
oracle.}

Figure 1 defines an algorithm called IsMessy that identifies messy
public keys. It has two essential properties (putting aside some very
rare exceptional cases):

\emph{•} IsMessy outputs ``messy'' on an overwhelming fraction of all
public keys \textbf{p}.

\emph{•} If IsMessy outputs ``messy'' on a particular public key
\textbf{p}, then \textbf{p} is indeed messy.

Note that IsMessy has one-sided error; it might output ``not sure'' on
some public keys \textbf{p} that are actually messy. Nevertheless,
\emph{most} messy keys are identified as such, and this is good enough
for the oblivious transfer application in {[}PVW07{]}.

Before analyzing IsMessy, let us define GOOD to be the set of common
matrices \textbf{A} \emph{∈}Z\emph{n×m} whose columns generate Z\emph{n
q}(i.e., the rows are linearly independent over Z\emph{q}) and
\emph{λ∞}1(Λ(\textbf{A})) \emph{≥q/}4. By Lemmas 5.1 and 5.3, all but an
at most 2\emph{q−n}fraction of matrices \textbf{A} are in GOOD when
\emph{m ≥}2(\emph{n} + 1) lg \emph{q}. The next two lemmas establish the
two main properties of the IsMessy algorithm. We first show that for all
\textbf{A} \emph{∈}GOOD, IsMessy outputs ``messy'' (with overwhelming
probability) on almost all public keys \textbf{p}.
\end{quote}

