\begin{quote}
We also show that it is possible to extract the secret key \textbf{s}
from a properly-generated public key \textbf{p} =
\textbf{A}\emph{T}\textbf{s} + \textbf{x} for Regev's cryptosystem,
using a trapdoor for the shared matrix \textbf{A}. Extracting secret
keys is equivalent to solving a bounded-distance (unique) decoding
problem on the lattice Λ(\textbf{A}). Whereas distinguishing messy keys
from properly-generated ones is a \emph{decision} problem, extracting
the secret key from a public key is essentially the correspoding
\emph{search} problem.

Building on the techniques of Aharonov and Regev {[}AR05{]}, Liu,
Lyubashevsky, and Micciancio {[}LLM06{]} gave a deterministic
``hill-climbing'' algorithm that solves the bounded-distance decoding
problem on any lattice, given samples from a discrete Gaussian over the
dual lattice. Their algorithm was generalized to all \emph{ℓp} norms in
{[}Pei07{]}.

\textbf{Proposition 8.8} ({[}LLM06, Pei07{]})\textbf{.} \emph{There is a
deterministic poly-time oracle algorithm that, given:}

\emph{• access to an oracle that samples from the distribution
D}Λ\emph{∗,s for some s \textgreater{}} 0\emph{,} \emph{• a basis}
\textbf{B} \emph{for a full-rank lattice} Λ = \emph{L}(\textbf{B})
\emph{⊂}R\emph{msuch that λ∞}1(Λ) \emph{≥}10\emph{√}log \emph{m/s,}
\emph{• a point} \textbf{x} \emph{∈}R\emph{msuch that}
dist\emph{∞}(\textbf{x}\emph{,} Λ)
\emph{≤}1\emph{/}(10\emph{s√m})\emph{, and}\\
\emph{outputs the unique} \textbf{y} \emph{∈}Λ \emph{closest (in ℓ∞norm)
to} \textbf{x}\emph{.}
\end{quote}

As we have already seen, for a random lattice Λ = Λ(\textbf{A}) with
Λ\emph{∗}= Λ\emph{⊥}(\textbf{A})\emph{/q}, we can implement the oracle
in the above lemma for any \emph{s} = \emph{L/q · ω}(\emph{√}log
\emph{m}) using the SampleD algorithm with any good basis
