\begin{quote}
The results of this section are very similar to the main worst-case to
average-case reduction of Micciancio and Regev {[}MR07{]} (which in turn
inherits from the original work of Ajtai {[}Ajt96{]}). The main
difference is the use of our discrete Gaussian sampling algorithm to
simplify and slightly tighten the reduction. The discrete sampling
algorithm avoids certain complications associated with using
\emph{continuous} Gaussian distributions, and the looseness that comes
with ``rounding off'' real-valued samples to nearby lattice points. The
net effect is that our reduction works for values of the modulus
\emph{q} = ˜\emph{O}(\emph{n}) that are almost linear,
versus˜\emph{O}(\emph{n}2) as shown in {[}MR07{]}.

We start by introducing a worst-case lattice problem that acts as an
intermediary between our average-case decoding problem and more standard
problems like SIVP and GapSVP. The new problem is similar to the
intermediate \emph{incremental guaranteed distance decoding} (IncGDD)
problem defined in {[}MR07{]}, but has a somewhat simpler formulation.

\textbf{Definition 9.1} (Incremental Independent Vectors
Decoding)\textbf{.} An input to IncIVD\emph{φγ,g}is a tuple
(\textbf{B}\emph{,} \textbf{S}\emph{,} \textbf{t}), where \textbf{B} is
a basis for a full-rank lattice in R\emph{n}, \textbf{S}
\emph{⊂L}(\textbf{B}) is a full-rank set of lattice vectors such
that\emph{∥}\textbf{S}\emph{∥≥γ}(\emph{n}) \emph{· φ}(\textbf{B}), and
\textbf{t} \emph{∈}R\emph{n}is a target point. The goal is to output a
lattice vector \textbf{v} \emph{∈L}(\textbf{B}) such
that\emph{∥}\textbf{v} \emph{−}\textbf{t}\emph{∥≤∥}\textbf{S}\emph{∥/g}.
(The norm \emph{∥·∥}is arbitrary, and is implicitly taken to be the
Euclidean \emph{ℓ}2 norm unless otherwise specified.)
\end{quote}

35

\begin{quote}
As shown in {[}MR07{]}, the IncGDD problem is as hard as approximating
several other worst-case problems (such as SIVP), via standard
worst-case to worst-case reductions. All of these reductions can easily
be adapted to work for our problem IncIVD as well. Therefore it will
suffice to show that IncIVD reduces to the average-case problem SIS or
ISIS for appropriate choices of parameters.

Before stating the main theorem, we observe that SIS can be viewed
essentially as a special case of ISIS. An instance of ISIS is given by
(\emph{q,} \textbf{A}\emph{,} \textbf{u}\emph{, β}) where \textbf{A}
\emph{∈}Z\emph{n×m} and \textbf{u} \emph{∈}Z\emph{n q}are uniformly
random, and the goal is to find an \textbf{e} \emph{∈}Z\emph{m}such that
\textbf{Ae} = \textbf{u} mod \emph{q} and \emph{∥}\textbf{e}\emph{∥≤β}.
Note that \textbf{u} \emph{̸}= \textbf{0} except with \emph{q−n}
probability, so without loss of generality we can assume that a valid
solution \textbf{e} \emph{̸}= \textbf{0}. Now by instead always setting
\textbf{u} = \textbf{0} and explicitly requiring \textbf{e} \emph{̸}=
\textbf{0}, we get exactly the homogeneous SIS problem. Therefore, in
the reduction we will choose \textbf{u} \emph{∈}Z\emph{n}\\
\emph{q}to be either uniform or \textbf{0} as appropriate, and the
analysis will only use the hypothesis that the solution \textbf{e}
\emph{̸}= \textbf{0}.
\end{quote}

\textbf{Theorem 9.2.} \emph{For any g}(\emph{n}) \emph{\textgreater{}} 1
\emph{and negligible ϵ}(\emph{n})\emph{, there is a probabilistic
poly-time reduction from solving} IncIVD\emph{ηϵaverage with
